# Lords of the Forest

In a forest so dark that even the sun feared to enter lived bears, tigers, wolves, crocodiles, vultures, and a dwindling herd of deer that survived mainly to become prey. A hunter once captured a wilful tiger cub. A rich man bought it, caged it in his mansion, and raised it until its roar terrified everyone. The master falsely told a woman beside him that he had killed its mother and rescued the orphan. His paid servant knew better, but his wages required him to turn the master's lie into truth.

One night the household and guards were drunk. One mistook the tiger's roar for music; another, pitying the lone creature amid their pleasure, opened its cage. The tiger raced back to the forest. The animals welcomed it and asked about the human country. It replied that the forest ought to bow its head in shame: beasts labour and risk injury merely to find food, while mansion-dwellers consume delicacies without labour and waste the excess. Beasts kill with tooth and claw; humans can kill with words. Beasts sleep when hunger is satisfied; humans remain awake to ruin whole communities. Even birds and dancers there perform at the command of money.

Troubled by all it had seen, the tiger undertook penance and summoned Shiva. When the god offered a boon, it asked only for understanding. Tigers, it said, hide, leap, tear, drink blood, and kill because that is the nature assigned to them. They live in darkness and face retaliation from other animals, floods, fire, falling trees, and hunters. They accept this ordained condition.

But what of humans? They violate women, commit murder, theft, lust, gambling and drunkenness, and drive the vulnerable toward ruin. Tigers kill for hunger; humans kill for taste, greed, and power. Were Valluvar and the teachers of virtue sent into the forest? No, they taught in the human country. Where are books on righteousness read and governments formed to preserve order? Among humans. Again and again God sent saints to guide them, while doing almost nothing to alter the beasts' given nature. Yet humans ignore all that care and submit to every impulse.

The tiger asked why creatures that openly follow their assigned nature are confined to the forest, while humans who possess reason, scripture, law, government, and moral instruction may practise cruelties far exceeding those of beasts and still claim civilisation. Why are the beasts named savage and humans named noble? If the marks of beastliness are violence, predation, deceit, and destruction, who truly owns the forest?

The question exposed the injustice hidden inside familiar names. The animals inhabit the wilderness by necessity; human beings can carry a darker wilderness within organised society. Those who possess knowledge but refuse compassion are more dangerous than tooth and claw. The tiger sought no wealth or power, only an answer: are we the lords of the forest, or are they?
